      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask how the bunion affects her day, which shoes she can wear, and what she hopes treatment will achieve.\n\nExplain what a bunion is in simple words: the big toe joint drifting out of line, with a bony bump on the side.\n\nExplain that non-surgical options can ease pain and rubbing but will not straighten the toe.\n\nDiscuss footwear: a wide, deep toe area, soft uppers over the bump, a low heel and laces or straps.\n\nOffer gel or felt padding over the bump, and explain that toe spacers and night splints may feel comfortable but do not correct the bone.\n\nDiscuss whether an insole might help, depending on how her foot moves when she walks, and simple pain relief as the packet directs.\n\nExplain that surgery is an option if pain persists despite these measures, and that it is done for pain, not appearance.\n\nDescribe recovery honestly: usually around six weeks in a special post-operative shoe, limited driving, and several months before swelling fully settles.\n\nExplore what she needs to keep doing each week, including any caring or driving, and help her weigh the options against it.\n\nAgree a plan, offering referral to an orthopaedic surgeon now or later, and check her understanding of both paths.\n\nTell her to get in touch if the joint becomes hot, red and swollen, or if a sore develops over the bump."
